{"hierarchy":{"paths":[["doc:\/\/Traversal\/documentation\/Traversal","doc:\/\/Traversal\/documentation\/Traversal\/GraphProtocols","doc:\/\/Traversal\/documentation\/Traversal\/GraphProtocols\/Graph"]]},"schemaVersion":{"major":0,"minor":3,"patch":0},"kind":"symbol","metadata":{"roleHeading":"Instance Method","role":"symbol","symbolKind":"method","modules":[{"name":"Traversal","relatedModules":["GraphProtocols"]}],"extendedModule":"GraphProtocols","externalID":"s:14GraphProtocols0A0P9TraversalE31lexicographicBreadthFirstSearchSay6VertexQzGyF","remoteSource":{"url":"https:\/\/github.com\/Tyler-Keith-Thompson\/Grafluent\/blob\/main\/Sources\/Traversal\/ChordalOrderings.swift#L181","fileName":"ChordalOrderings.swift"},"title":"lexicographicBreadthFirstSearch()","fragments":[{"kind":"keyword","text":"func"},{"kind":"text","text":" "},{"kind":"identifier","text":"lexicographicBreadthFirstSearch"},{"kind":"text","text":"() -> ["},{"kind":"typeIdentifier","text":"Self"},{"kind":"text","text":"."},{"preciseIdentifier":"s:14GraphProtocols0A0P6VertexQa","kind":"typeIdentifier","text":"Vertex"},{"kind":"text","text":"]"}]},"primaryContentSections":[{"kind":"declarations","declarations":[{"tokens":[{"text":"func","kind":"keyword"},{"text":" ","kind":"text"},{"text":"lexicographicBreadthFirstSearch","kind":"identifier"},{"text":"() -> [","kind":"text"},{"text":"Self","kind":"typeIdentifier"},{"text":".","kind":"text"},{"text":"Vertex","kind":"typeIdentifier","preciseIdentifier":"s:14GraphProtocols0A0P6VertexQa","identifier":"doc:\/\/GraphProtocols\/documentation\/GraphProtocols\/Graph\/Vertex"},{"text":"]","kind":"text"}],"platforms":["Linux"],"languages":["swift"]}]},{"content":[{"level":2,"type":"heading","anchor":"discussion","text":"Discussion"},{"inlineContent":[{"type":"text","text":"Read on the simple graph: self-loops and parallel copies change nothing. The reverse of the"},{"type":"text","text":" "},{"type":"text","text":"order is a perfect elimination ordering exactly when the graph is chordal."}],"type":"paragraph"},{"style":"note","content":[{"type":"paragraph","inlineContent":[{"type":"text","text":"O(n + m), by partition refinement."}]}],"type":"aside","name":"Complexity"}],"kind":"content"}],"abstract":[{"type":"text","text":"Every vertex in an order a lexicographic breadth-first search (LexBFS; Rose, Tarjan and"},{"type":"text","text":" "},{"type":"text","text":"Lueker) can visit them, starting at the first vertex in "},{"code":"vertices","type":"codeVoice"},{"type":"text","text":" order. A vertex’s label"},{"type":"text","text":" "},{"type":"text","text":"is the visit positions of its visited neighbors, in increasing order; each next vertex has"},{"type":"text","text":" "},{"type":"text","text":"a greatest label, where a label is greater when it has the smaller position at the first"},{"type":"text","text":" "},{"type":"text","text":"difference or extends the other. Which of several equal labels goes first is not"},{"type":"text","text":" "},{"type":"text","text":"specified; the order is the same for the same graph and the same rows."}],"variants":[{"traits":[{"interfaceLanguage":"swift"}],"paths":["\/documentation\/traversal\/graphprotocols\/graph\/lexicographicbreadthfirstsearch()"]}],"identifier":{"url":"doc:\/\/Traversal\/documentation\/Traversal\/GraphProtocols\/Graph\/lexicographicBreadthFirstSearch()","interfaceLanguage":"swift"},"sections":[],"references":{"doc://GraphProtocols/documentation/GraphProtocols/Graph/Vertex":{"url":"\/documentation\/graphprotocols\/graph\/vertex","title":"Vertex","identifier":"doc:\/\/GraphProtocols\/documentation\/GraphProtocols\/Graph\/Vertex","type":"topic","fragments":[{"text":"associatedtype","kind":"keyword"},{"text":" ","kind":"text"},{"text":"Vertex","kind":"identifier"},{"text":" : ","kind":"text"},{"text":"Hashable","kind":"typeIdentifier","preciseIdentifier":"s:SH"}],"kind":"symbol","abstract":[],"role":"symbol"},"doc://Traversal/documentation/Traversal":{"role":"collection"